While I can certainly reproduce your specific issue it is also code that is unsafe in concurrent mode since you're triggering side-effects in render. Once you switch to 

```js
React.useEffect(() => {
  cbRef.current = () => {
    setState((c) => String.fromCharCode(c.charCodeAt(0) + 1));
  };
}, []);
```
devtools is no longer interfering.

Do you have a use case that isn't triggering side-effects during render? Adding the original use case would help the maintainers decide if this is an issue worth working on.

Hi, can you elaborate on why it is considered side-effect? AFAIK it's just a variable assignment to the ref.

```js
cbRef.current = () => {
  setState(c => String.fromCharCode(c.charCodeAt(0) + 1))
}
```

The original use case is from [`useSubscription`](https://github.com/crimx/observable-hooks/blob/master/packages/observable-hooks/src/internal/use-subscription-internal.ts) in observable-hooks. It has a signature of `useSubscription(stream$, next, error, complete)`, where the `stream$` is an observable, and the rest are observer callback functions.

Under the hood `useSubscription` uses a ref object to keep the latest callbacks so that users don't have to worry about closure staleness and can reference any variable directly in the callbacks like in `useEffect`. This should be safe in concurrent mode but correct me if I am wrong.

In [useObservableSuspense](https://github.com/crimx/observable-hooks/blob/16fc97c1058eefffe6c0f7cd646ce8995e2e8de6/packages/observable-hooks/src/use-observable-suspense.ts), usage like this causes the issue:

```js
const [state, setState] = useState(0)

useSubscription(stream$, value => {
  setState(value)
})
```

Currently I fixed this by keeping the initial `setState`.

```js
const [state, _setState] = useState(0)
const setState = useRef(_setState).current

useSubscription(stream$, value => {
  setState(value)
})
```

The only case when mutating a ref during render is safe is [the lazy-initialization case](https://reactjs.org/docs/hooks-faq.html#how-to-create-expensive-objects-lazily) shown in the docs.

The "bug" being reported here seems to be caused by an unsafe mutation during render. From what I can tell, there is no other bug here. (Is that right? Am I overlooking something?)

> It's a mutation in render, which makes it impure. That can cause issues if a render doesn't end up getting committed to the screen. (However, in this particular case it doesn't seem like it would matter since you're not using any props/state in the closure.)

I think how this would break things (in what cases) depends on how the ref is passed along.

```js
function BadExampleA() {
  const [state, setState] = React.useState("A");
  const cbRef = React.useRef(null);

  // This is a mutation during render; it is not safe!
  cbRef.current = value => setState(value);

  // This might not break things,
  // since the updated event handler won't be applied/attached.
  // However if there are any timers or other async code that closes over the ref,
  // those things would be broken by the unsafe mutation.
  return <button onClick={cbRef.current}>button</button>;
}

function BadExampleB() {
  const [state, setState] = React.useState("A");
  const cbRef = React.useRef(null);

  // This is a mutation during render; it is not safe!
  cbRef.current = value => setState(value);

  // This would break things,
  // because even though the new inline event handler is discarded,
  // the previous one still closes over the ref's current value.
  // Similarly if there are other functions that close over the ref's value
  // e.g in custom hooks, they would also break.
  return <button onClick={() => cbRef.current()}>button</button>;
}
```

> One more question regarding concurrent mode. Is it safe/recommended to use layout effects for this purpose?
>
> useIsomorphicLayoutEffect(() => {
>   argsRef.current = args
> })

You could use an effect or a layout effect. Our [docs example](https://reactjs.org/docs/hooks-faq.html#how-to-get-the-previous-props-or-state) uses an effect.

I'm going to close this issue because I don't think there's anything wrong here. Please let me know if I've misread or misunderstood something though and I can take another look.
